# Documentation (Zensical)

Everything we build gets documented in `docs/`, built with [Zensical](https://zensical.org/) — the successor to Material for MkDocs by the same team. Docs are code: versioned in git, updated in the **same change set** as the feature, reviewed with it.

## Two audiences, two trees (per language)

Languages live under `docs/en/` and `docs/de/`. Zensical builds them separately to `site/en/` and `site/de/` (language selector via `project.extra.alternate`). For the DE site, `build-docs.bash` copies `docs/en/` into `.docs-build/de/` and lays `docs/de/` over it, so a page without a DE file shows up **in English inside the German menu**.

- A new or changed page gets both `docs/en/` and `docs/de/` in the same change set; nav entries go into `zensical.toml`, `zensical.en.toml` (English labels) and `zensical.de.toml` (German labels).
- Anchors differ per language (headings are slugified). Links between DE pages use the German anchors.

- `docs/<lang>/user/` — **user documentation**: how to *use* the software, frontend/GUI focused. Task-oriented step-by-step guides ("How to create a report"), screenshots of the UI, FAQs. No internals, no code, no jargon; write for a non-developer.
- `docs/<lang>/technical/` — **technical documentation**: how it's *built*, developer/backend focused. Architecture overview, setup/installation, API reference, data model, deployment, decisions and trade-offs. Complete, copy-paste-ready code examples.

Never mix audiences in one page. `docs/<lang>/index.md` is the landing page linking both trees. Hosted technical paths (`/en/technical`, `/de/technical`) require admin SSO.

## README vs docs/

`README.md` is the shop window, `docs/` is the shop. The README stays a short introduction — everything substantial lives in `docs/` and is linked, never duplicated:

- README sections, in order: title + one-liner (what it is, who it's for), optional screenshot/demo, quickstart (minimal install + run, a few commands max), key features, tech stack, link to the full documentation, license.
- No walls of text, no dev journal, no detailed how-tos, no API or architecture content — those belong in `docs/user/` and `docs/technical/`.
- If a README section grows beyond ~10 lines, move the content to `docs/` and leave a link.
- Keep the quickstart runnable; an outdated README command is a bug like any other stale doc.

## Setup and structure

This project uses `zensical.toml` / `zensical.en.toml` / `zensical.de.toml` (settings under `[project]`). Build both languages with `./01_scripts/global/build-docs.bash` (Docker image `zensical/zensical`). Hosted docs: `core--docs--app` at `docs.<SCS_SUBDOMAIN>.<SCS_BASE_DOMAIN>`.

```
zensical.toml / zensical.en.toml / zensical.de.toml
docs/
  en/                 # English source of truth
    index.md
    user/
    technical/
  de/                 # German overlays (user guide + translated pages)
site/                 # build output (gitignored): index.html + en/ + de/
```

Minimal language config:

```toml
[project]
site_name = "My Project"
docs_dir = "docs/en"
site_dir = "site/en"
site_url = "https://docs.example.org/en/"
nav = [
  "index.md",
  {"User Guide" = ["user/index.md"]},
  {"Technical" = ["technical/index.md"]},
]

[project.extra]
alternate = [
  { name = "English", link = "/en/", lang = "en" },
  { name = "Deutsch", link = "/de/", lang = "de" },
]

[project.markdown_extensions.admonition]
[project.markdown_extensions.pymdownx.details]
[project.markdown_extensions.pymdownx.superfences]
[project.markdown_extensions.pymdownx.tabbed]
alternate_style = true
```

- Register every page in `nav`; keep nav order task-frequency-first for user docs, onboarding-first (setup before architecture) for technical docs.
- Inverted pyramid per page: what/why first, steps next, background last. One `#` H1 per page, then `##`/`###` — never skip levels.

## Markdown syntax

Zensical keeps the Material for MkDocs syntax. Use its features instead of plain-markdown workarounds:

```markdown
!!! warning "Data loss"
    Deleting a project removes all its reports.

??? info "Details for the curious"
    Collapsed by default — background info goes here.

=== "Linux"
    ```bash
    ./install.sh
    ```
=== "macOS"
    ```bash
    brew install foo
    ```
```

- Admonitions (`!!! note|tip|warning|danger`) for callouts, `???` for collapsible detail (`???+` starts expanded) — indent content by 4 spaces.
- Content tabs (`=== "Label"`) for OS/language/role alternatives.
- Fenced code blocks always carry a language; use `mermaid` fences for diagrams instead of committed images where possible.

## Keeping docs current

- A change that alters behavior, UI, API, or setup updates the affected pages in the same change set — stale docs are bugs.
- Update screenshots when the UI changes; keep code examples runnable.
- Deprecated features: mark with a `!!! warning "Deprecated"` admonition, link the replacement and migration steps — don't delete the page while users may still be on the old version.
